const TV = {"ocean": {"sec": [{"t": "Sea Surface Temperature (SST)", "p": ["Sea surface temperature (SST) plays a key role in air-sea heat exchange, but infrared satellite observations are subject to observation gaps caused by clouds and noise, while numerical models are computationally intensive and take a long time to reflect real-time conditions. CARE-SST and PARAN are two deep learning techniques that fuse physical and background information with satellite observation data to reconstruct high-resolution SST distributions without observational gaps.", "CARE-SST: a context-aware denoising diffusion model that fuses VIIRS infrared imagery with OISST background fields to produce global, daily, ~2 km gap-filled SST, recovering fine-scale currents and fronts even under heavy cloud cover.", "PARAN: a Physics-Assisted Reconstruction Adversarial Network combining Himawari-8 geostationary data with physical drivers (solar radiation, wind speed) via GAN to generate continuous, 2 km, hourly SST over the Northwest Pacific (2019–2021), accurately capturing diurnal warming."], "refs": ["Choo et al., 2025", "Jung et al., 2025"], "prog": false, "img": "ocean-2", "tr": ["해수면 온도(SST)", "海表温度（SST）"], "rid": [36, 29]}, {"t": "Sea Surface Salinity (SSS)", "p": ["Sea Surface Salinity (SSS) is a key parameter of the ocean, and it serves as a proxy for climate change by interacting directly with the atmosphere. We are monitoring and predicting SSS in the Northeast Asia Sea region based on artificial intelligence (AI) methods. We are using the fusion of multiple satellite, reanalysis models, and in-situ data. From our studies, the SSS of the Northeast Asia Sea can be monitored more accurately, and it can reduce damage from low salinity water by predicting SSS in summer.", "Effective, novel gap-filling method for generating bias-corrected global daily SSS without delay", "Using machine learning approaches, generate high resolution SSS from Geostationary Ocean Color Imager (GOCI) data"], "refs": ["Jung et al., 2025", "Sung et al., 2025"], "prog": false, "img": "ocean-3", "tr": ["해수면 염분(SSS)", "海表盐度（SSS）"], "rid": [29, 34]}, {"t": "Coastal Water Quality", "p": ["Monitoring water quality over coastal areas is important as most industrial facilities and residential areas are located along the coast. There are several representative water quality parameters, such as chlorophyll-a, dissolved oxygen, and suspended solids. Field-measured water quality parameters do not provide spatial and temporal continuity, so satellite remote sensing can be an alternative for identifying surface water quality. We are monitoring coastal water quality around the Korean Peninsula based on artificial intelligence (AI) methods. From our studies, the water quality of South Korea can be monitored continuously, and hourly available Geostationary Ocean Color Imager (GOCI) images are useful to discuss spatiotemporal distributions of the water quality parameters.", "Estimating water quality index (WQI) around the Korean Peninsula", "Analyzing the Spatio-temporal variability of water quality", "Developing a high-resolution Forel-Ule-Index (FUI) calculation algorithm for Korean waters using observational data and machine learning techniques"], "refs": ["Jang et al., 2016", "Sung et al., 2021"], "prog": false, "img": "ocean-4", "tr": ["연안 수질", "近岸水质"], "rid": [191, null]}, {"t": "Harmful Algal Blooms", "p": ["The harmful algal blooms (HABs), or red tide, are one of the significant coastal disasters worldwide, which causes large socio-economic damages in the fishery and aquaculture industries. However, red tide water, which has high red and infra-red reflectance, often causes failure in atmospheric correction, making quantification algorithms based on remote sensing reflectance produce large errors. We are to develop a red tide detection algorithm using satellite data based on machine learning."], "refs": ["Kwon et al., 2018", "Kim et al., 2023"], "prog": false, "img": "ocean-5", "tr": ["유해 조류 대발생", "有害藻华"], "rid": [172, 82]}, {"t": "Blue carbon", "p": ["An acceleration of climate change in recent years has led to increased attention towards 'blue carbon' which refers to the carbon captured by the ocean. The monitoring of ocean carbon dioxide is important for a better understanding of the role of the ocean as a carbon sink, and regional and global carbon cycles. We estimated the surface seawater ƒCO2 in the East Sea of Korea using in situ measurements, GOCI satellite and their derived products, and HYCOM reanalysis data through machine learning approaches.", "To understand marine ecosystems, we classified and analyzed global marine eco-provinces using k-means clustering considering carbon cycling. The findings can also be employed in the development of guidelines for the systematic management of vulnerable coastal regions to climate change.", "Estimation of the fugacity of carbon dioxide (fCO2) over the East Sea using in situ measurements, satellite data, products from the GOCI and HYCOM reanalysis data", "Classification and analysis of global marine eco-provinces using k-means clustering, considering carbon cycling"], "refs": ["Jang et al., 2017", "Kim et al., 2023"], "prog": false, "img": "ocean-6", "tr": ["블루카본", "蓝碳"], "rid": [175, 82]}, {"t": "Blue carbon: 3-D chlorophyll-a", "p": ["Blue carbon — carbon captured and stored by ocean ecosystems — plays a key role in mitigating climate change, and chlorophyll-a (Chl-a), the primary pigment of phytoplankton photosynthesis, serves as a core indicator of the ocean's carbon sequestration capacity. However, sparse in-situ observations and the ocean's complex physical dynamics have long made it difficult to reconstruct the global 3-D (vertical) distribution of Chl-a. Existing AI- and numerical-model-based approaches tend to underestimate or smooth out depth-dependent variability such as the subsurface chlorophyll maximum (SCM).", "Hybrid ML framework combining satellite ocean color, 3-D physical reanalysis, and BGC-Argo profiles — PCM classifies Chl-a profile shapes and LGBM estimates structural parameters (SCM depth, width) — to reconstruct global 3-D Chl-a at 0.25° resolution (R² = 0.821, RMSE = 0.092 mg/m³).", "By capturing vertical profile geometry, the model overcomes the smoothing/underestimation bias of prior neural network and numerical models, improving global ocean productivity and biological carbon pump estimates."], "refs": ["Cho et al., 2026"], "prog": false, "img": "ocean-7", "tr": ["블루카본: 3차원 엽록소-a", "蓝碳：三维叶绿素a"], "rid": [2]}, {"t": "Ocean-fog", "p": ["Ocean-fog is a kind of cloud that attaches its base to the ocean surface, with a large number of droplets that lowers the visibility of less than 1km. Since 2013, about 60 accidents caused by low-visibility have occurred annually over the Korean ocean; thus, the detection and prediction of the ocean-fog are important to prevent socio-economic losses. We use a combination of station measurement, satellite products, and numerical weather prediction (NWP) model products in conjunction with deep-learning techniques to detect and predict ocean-fog.", "For ocean–fog detection via the Himawari-8 satellite using machine-learning and an objective procedure, regardless of whether it is day or night", "Advanced ocean fog prediction model for the Yellow Sea region, leveraging satellite-based detection and high-performance data-driven methods"], "refs": ["Sim et al., 2023", "Sim et al., 2024"], "prog": false, "img": "ocean-8", "tr": ["해무", "海雾"], "rid": [70, 45]}], "pubs": [2, 29, 34, 36, 43, 45, 70, 82, 98, 102, 125, 172, 175, 191, 195, 209], "ov": [["Physical oceanography", "물리 해양학", "物理海洋学", "SST, SSS", "ic_Ocean_0"], ["Biochemical oceanography", "생지화학 해양학", "生物化学海洋学", "Coastal water quality, Harmful algal blooms, Blue carbon", "ic_Ocean_1"], ["Air-sea interaction", "해양-대기 상호작용", "海气相互作用", "Ocean fog", "ic_Ocean_2"]]}, "airquality": {"sec": [{"t": "Hourly Aerosol Optical Depth Retrieval", "p": ["Atmospheric aerosols are not only air pollutants that are harmful to human health, but they also play an important role in understanding climate change. As aerosol optical depth (AOD) provides a quantitative estimate of the aerosols in the atmosphere, it can be used as an indicator of air quality. Due to the limited spatial distribution of ground-based observation stations, continuous monitoring is challenging, prompting the widespread use of satellite-based AOD monitoring. Geostationary satellites are particularly advantageous for real-time and accurate monitoring of aerosols with high spatiotemporal variability. Monitoring aerosols with spatial variations in environmental factors can provide better understanding of the spatiotemporal patterns. Therefore, we aimed to accurately retrieve hourly AOD by integrating the use of geostationary satellites with environmental variables. Artificial intelligence techniques were employed to retrieve hourly AOD using various input features, evaluating the effectiveness of spatial information and identifying key variables driving AOD variability. GOCI geostationary satellite data were used to analyze the influence of spatiotemporal weather variations on AOD fluctuations. Furthermore, an algorithm utilizing hyperspectral data from the GEMS satellite was developed to extend AOD monitoring across broader regions."], "refs": ["Kang et al., 2022"], "prog": false, "img": "wix-aq", "tr": ["시간별 에어로솔 광학두께 산출", "逐小时气溶胶光学厚度反演"], "rid": [105]}], "pubs": [12, 22, 23, 27, 32, 37, 40, 74, 76, 80, 95, 104, 105, 108, 142, 147, 161], "ov": [["Aerosol optical depth retrieval", "에어로솔 광학두께 산출", "气溶胶光学厚度反演", "Hourly AOD, Nighttime AOD, High-resolution AOD", "ic_Air_Quality_0"], ["Surface-level air pollutant estimation", "지상 대기오염물질 추정", "近地面空气污染物估算", "Real-time learning, Particulate matter (PM), Gaseous pollutants", "ic_Air_Quality_1"]]}, "carbon": {"sec": [{"t": "Carbon Flux", "p": ["Global warming and climate change have significantly altered vegetation ecosystems, affecting how plants perform photosynthesis. Monitoring these biological processes is crucial for understanding the Earth’s carbon cycle and informing climate policies, yet accurately tracking them remains challenging due to the large spatiotemporal variability in vegetation responses to external factors. This study hypothesizes that incorporating environmental factors can effectively represent changes in carbon exchange processes.", "In this research, we use machine learning and deep learning models to estimate gross primary productivity (GPP), ecosystem respiration (Reco), and net ecosystem exchange (NEE) based on various input features, analyzing which variables most impact plant carbon exchange. We are conducting two separate studies utilizing data from polar-orbiting and geostationary satellites to leverage their respective spatial and temporal advantages in monitoring terrestrial carbon flux. This approach demonstrates the potential of satellite observations combined with environmental variables to analyze how external factors influence GPP variations."], "refs": ["Bae et al., 2025", "Bae et al., 2026"], "prog": false, "img": "carbon-2", "tr": ["탄소 플럭스", "碳通量"], "rid": [28, 15]}, {"t": "Carbon storage", "p": ["Vegetation carbon storage is crucial in mitigating climate change by capturing atmospheric carbon dioxide in plant biomass. Monitoring carbon stock is essential for understanding how vegetation contributes to the global carbon cycle and ecosystem health. Satellite data enables efficient, large-scale tracking of vegetation and carbon dynamics, providing accurate assessments of carbon sequestration potential to inform climate policies.", "We have specifically used high-resolution satellite imagery to assess vegetation biomass and carbon storages not only in forests but also in urban areas. Urban vegetation, although often fragmented and heterogeneous, plays an important role in improving environmental quality and enhancing carbon sequestration within cities. Achieving carbon neutrality requires sustainable green infrastructure in urban environments, as well as continuous monitoring to support effective management and planning. Using airborne Light Detection and Ranging (LiDAR), satellite multispectral imagery, and Geographic Information System (GIS) data, we applied AI techniques to estimate urban vegetation carbon stock (Lee et al., 2024)."], "refs": ["Lee et al., 2018", "Lee et al., 2024"], "prog": false, "img": "carbon-3", "tr": ["탄소 저장량", "碳储量"], "rid": [166, 49]}, {"t": "Vegetation monitoring", "p": ["Satellite remote sensing provides a powerful approach for vegetation monitoring by exploiting the spectral characteristics of vegetation. These spectral signals enable continuous monitoring of vegetation condition over large areas, which is essential for understanding dynamic changes in vegetation health. In our research, we monitor vegetation conditions using various vegetation indices derived from satellite imagery, including widely used indicators such as the Normalized Difference Vegetation Index (NDVI) and Leaf Area Index (LAI). In particular, LAI was corrected using the woody-to-total-area ratio (α) derived from satellite reflectance, thereby compensating for the overestimation problem in field observations (Kim et al., 2025).", "Understanding land surface structure is also important for characterizing vegetation distribution. In this context, we address problems such as land cover classification to better represent surface characteristics. We also develop methods to extract detailed vegetation information in urban environments that is not typically provided in conventional land cover products, including urban tree canopy cover segmentation (Son et al., 2021), individual tree detection (Lee et al., 2023), and fractional tree canopy cover (FTC) maps (Bae et al., 2024)."], "refs": ["Kim et al., 2025", "Son et al., 2021", "Lee et al., 2023", "Bae et al., 2024"], "prog": false, "img": "carbon-4", "tr": ["식생 모니터링", "植被监测"], "rid": [21, 119, 75, null]}], "pubs": [1, 15, 21, 28, 48, 49, 54, 75, 119, 157, 166, 192, 207], "ov": [["Carbon fluxes", "탄소 플럭스", "碳通量", "Gross primary productivity (GPP), Ecosystem respiration (Reco), Net ecosystem exchange (NEE)", "ic_Carbon_0"], ["Carbon storage", "탄소 저장량", "碳储量", "Vegetation carbon stock, Aboveground biomass", "ic_Carbon_1"], ["Vegetation monitoring", "식생 모니터링", "植被监测", "Leaf area index, Land surface structure (land cover, etc.)", "ic_Carbon_2"]]}, "atmosphere": {"sec": [{"t": "Tropical Cyclone Forecasting", "p": ["Tropical cyclone (TC) intensity change forecasting remains challenging due to the lack of understanding of the interactions between TC changes and environmental parameters, and the high uncertainties resulting from climate change. This study proposed hybrid convolutional neural networks (hybrid-CNN), which effectively combined satellite-based spatial characteristics and numerical prediction model outputs, to forecast TC intensity with lead times of 24, 48, and 72 h. The models were validated against best track data by TC category and phase and compared with the Korea Meteorological Administrator (KMA)-based TC forecasts. The hybrid-CNN-based forecasts outperformed KMA-based forecasts, exhibiting up to 22%, 110%, and 7% improvement in skill scores for the 24-, 48-, and 72-h forecasts, respectively. For rapid intensification cases, the models exhibited improvements of 62%, 87%, and 50% over KMA-based forecasts for the three lead times. Moreover, explainable deep learning demonstrated hybrid-CNN’s potential in predicting TC intensity and contributing to the TC forecasting field."], "refs": ["Lee et al., 2024"], "prog": false, "img": "wix-atm", "tr": ["태풍 예측", "热带气旋预报"], "rid": [47]}, {"t": "Precipitation Nowcasting", "p": ["This study proposes X2R, a region-specific multimodal dataset for South Korea integrating HSR radar, GK-2A satellite observations, and lightning data from the KMA, and HYDRO-X2R, a systematic comparative analysis framework built upon it.", "The proposed HYDRO-X2R framework utilizes a lightweight X2R PROJECTOR, enabling rigorous tracking of performance variations based on input modality availability while maintaining the same forecasting core. Specifically, four input configurations are defined by radar availability: R2R, M2R (radar-present) and S2R, SL2R (radar-absent).", "Furthermore, we propose a hybrid baseline combining the stable flow prediction of a deterministic model with the fine-grained detail generation of a residual diffusion model, striking an optimal balance between numerical accuracy and visual realism. Experimental results demonstrate that as lead time increases, satellite and lightning data play a crucial role in enhancing spatial robustness and effectively compensating for radar errors, highlighting the practical importance of multi-source data integration in environments with limited radar coverage or long-term forecasting scenarios.", "Qualitative precipitation forecasts at increasing lead times for a representative typhoon event, including ground-truth (GT) HSR. The left subfigure corresponds to a 2-hour nowcast produced at 10-minute intervals for the event at 2024-08-20 18:30 UTC, while the right subfigure corresponds to a 6-hour nowcast produced at 30-minute intervals for the event at 2024-08-20 14:00 UTC."], "refs": ["Ruzcko et al., 2026"], "prog": false, "img": "precip-1", "tr": ["초단기 강수 예측", "降水临近预报"], "rid": [null]}], "pubs": [3, 25, 47, 52, 93, 120, 148], "ov": [["Tropical cyclone", "태풍", "热带气旋", "TC forecasting, TC intensity estimation, TC center estimation", "ic_Atmosphere_new_0"], ["Precipitation", "강수", "降水", "Precipitation nowcasting, Overshooting top monitoring", "ic_Atmosphere_new_1"]]}, "urban": {"sec": [{"t": "Air Temperature", "p": ["Accurate temperature monitoring is critical to understanding and managing urban climate dynamics, especially in the context of rising global temperatures and increasing frequency of extreme events. However, due to the limited number of in situ air temperature monitoring stations, a significant gap exists in understanding detailed temperature patterns across cities.", "To address this problem, we utilize satellite-derived land surface temperature (LST) data, which provides comprehensive spatial coverage across urban terrain. We integrate machine learning techniques with LST data to reconstruct daily maximum and minimum temperatures for areas that cannot be directly measured.", "We also apply spatial interpolation methods to estimate air temperatures in unmonitored regions. We trained a machine learning model with satellite data and in situ observations to create a seamless spatial map of air temperature. Compared to the smoothed spatial distribution of the traditional geostatistical model, Cokriging, our model was found to better represent heterogeneous spatial patterns. This methodology enhances our ability to effectively monitor air temperature, helping us better understand and manage heat risks in cities."], "refs": ["Yoo et al., 2018", "Cho et al., 2020"], "prog": false, "img": "urban-2", "tr": ["기온", "气温"], "rid": [170, 138]}, {"t": "Land Surface Temperature", "p": ["Land surface temperature (LST) is a crucial variable in understanding the thermal dynamics of the Earth's surface. It directly influences surface heat fluxes, energy balance, and plays a significant role in shaping the local climate, especially in urban environments. Despite its importance, continuous and seamless monitoring of LST is challenging due to the data gaps in satellite-based LST measurements interrupted by cloud cover and the limited number of in-situ observation stations.", "To fill the incomplete LST measurements under cloudy conditions, first, we corrected biases between satellite-derived LST and in-situ measurements to minimize errors caused by cloud-induced cold or warm biases. Furthermore, we incorporated binary cloud cover data into the model, enabling us to better account for cloud-related impacts on LST and improve the overall reliability of our temperature estimates.", "Our research also explores innovative methods for hourly LST estimation using a geostationary satellite data from GEO-KOMPSAT-2A (GK2A). By leveraging accumulated longwave and shortwave radiation data from GK2A, we are able to capture the historical influence of cloud effects on surface temperatures. This allows us to estimate LST with higher precision and temporal granularity, providing valuable insights for real-time heat monitoring and urban climate management."], "refs": ["Yoo et al., 2020", "Cho et al., 2022"], "prog": false, "img": "urban-3", "tr": ["지표면 온도", "地表温度"], "rid": [129, 100]}, {"t": "LST Spatial Downscaling", "p": ["Satellite-based land surface temperature (LST) is a major parameter for analyzing urban thermal environments. However, due to the heterogeneity of urban spaces, there is a growing demand for high-resolution LST products to enable more detailed analyses. To address this need, we aim to develop a machine learning (ML)-based model for high-resolution LST estimation, referred to as LST spatial downscaling.", "We utilized various satellite data with a Random Forest (RF) model to downscale Moderate Resolution Imaging Spectroradiometer (MODIS) 1 km to 250 m. We demonstrated how high-resolution LST can reveal intra-urban LST variations based on land cover types. Additionally, we published a review paper that synthesized recent trends, limitations, and future research directions, expanding our understanding of LST spatial downscaling.", "RF is the most widely applied algorithm in LST downscaling, demonstrating robust performance.\tHowever, we noted the attenuation of extreme values and, in response to this issue, were the to apply Local Linear Forest (LLF) for spatial enhancement, demonstrating its efficacy."], "refs": ["Yoo et al., 2017", "Yoo et al., 2020", "Yoo et al., 2022"], "prog": false, "img": "urban-4", "tr": ["지표면 온도 공간 상세화", "地表温度空间降尺度"], "rid": [null, 129, 96]}, {"t": "Short-term Heatwave Forecasting", "p": ["Forecasts of maximum and minimum air temperatures are essential to mitigate the damage of extreme weather events such as heat waves and tropical nights. We aim to develop more accurate air temperature prediction models by correcting the prediction bias of numerical weather prediction models using various machine learning (ML) and deep learning (DL) based post‐processing techniques. Two numerical models provided by the Korean Meteorological Administration, LDAPS and GDAPS, are the targets.", "First, we developed three ML-based and one ensemble bias correction models for Seoul. The original LDAPS showed an R2 of 0.69 and a bias of -0.85℃ in the next day’s maximum temperature forecast. Our ensemble model showed improved forecast skill with an R2 of 0.78 and a bias of -0.13℃. Second, we applied DL to the bias correction model and expanded the study area to South Korea. Additionally, we developed a novel model that combines spatial downscaling and bias correction using a DL technique. In particular, we successfully predicted the daily minimum and maximum air temperature for 7 days across South Korea compared to GDAPS."], "refs": ["Cho et al., 2020", "Cho et al., 2022", "Cho et al., 2024"], "prog": false, "img": "urban-5", "tr": ["단기 폭염 예측", "短期热浪预报"], "rid": [138, 100, 50]}, {"t": "Long-term Heatwave Forecasting", "p": ["With climate change and frequent extreme weather events, long-term predictions of heat waves are becoming increasingly important for risk mitigation and disaster preparedness. We focused on distant ocean and land variability and their potential to influence South Korea's summer climate. This study identified global climate variables that are important for predicting heat waves in South Korea.", "We examined four climate components: soil moisture, snow depth, sea surface temperature, and sea ice concentration. Through long-term nonlinear correlation analysis, we selected a total of 16 winter or spring climate variabilities. Then, we built a machine learning-based heat wave prediction model using these variables as independent variables and the number of heat waves as the dependent variable. The model performed best when compared to one simple statistical model and one physics-based climate model. We evaluated the most important teleconnection drivers through explainable artificial intelligence (XAI) analysis and further analyzed their association with South Korea’s summer climate patterns and large-scale teleconnections which has already been demonstrated to be associated with the favorable condition for heatwaves in South Korea."], "refs": ["Lee et al., 2024"], "prog": false, "img": "urban-6", "tr": ["장기 폭염 예측", "长期热浪预报"], "rid": [42]}, {"t": "Local Climate Zone", "p": ["The Local Climate Zone (LCZ) scheme is a standardized classification system that represents the characteristics of urban forms and functions, particularly in urban heat island (UHI) research. It defines 17 distinct zones based on features such as building and tree height, density, and surface cover types, differentiating between pervious and impervious surfaces.", "We are the first to apply convolutional neural networks (CNNs) to LCZ mapping. Previous work has utilized satellite imagery and random forest (RF) classifiers for classification, but we significantly improved accuracy performance by using image-based deep learning technology.", "In addition, we proposed a method to improve the difficulty of classifying complex urban structures using satellite images alone by using geographic information system (GIS)-based building data. In particular, our framework selectively choose LCZ classification results based on the classification certainty of deep learning classifiers to reflect the limitations of incomplete building information.", "Our team remains committed to applying the LCZ framework to diverse studies, advancing sustainable urban development and environmental stewardship."], "refs": ["Yoo et al., 2019", "Yoo et al., 2020"], "prog": false, "img": "urban-7", "tr": ["국지 기후대(LCZ)", "局地气候区（LCZ）"], "rid": [151, 129]}, {"t": "Urban Thermal Environment Analysis", "p": ["As cities continue to grow and urbanize rapidly, the need to effectively monitor and manage the urban thermal environment becomes increasingly critical. The urban heat island (UHI) effect, combined with the impacts of climate change, presents significant challenges to public health, energy consumption, and urban sustainability. Understanding how urban structures and population dynamics contribute to heat distribution is essential for developing resilient cities.", "To address these challenges, we conducted a study in Suwon, South Korea, by comparing urban structure changes between 2004 and 2021 and its impact on LST. By employing LST downscaling techniques, we generated high-resolution (30 m) LST maps, which allowed us to closely examine thermal changes. We then analyzed the impact of urban expansion—both horizontal and vertical—on the thermal environment, providing valuable insights into how urban growth influences heat distribution across the city.", "We proposed a diurnal heat risk assessment framework that integrated spatiotemporal air temperature and real-time population data. Using machine learning techniques, we produced high-resolution (250 m) daily Tmax and Tmin maps, which better matched the actual heat exposure of the population and effectively showed the diurnal variation of heat risk between day and night."], "refs": ["Yoo et al., 2019", "Lee et al., 2021", "Lee et al., 2023", "Yoo et al., 2023"], "prog": false, "img": "urban-8", "tr": ["도시 열환경 분석", "城市热环境分析"], "rid": [151, 120, 75, 66]}, {"t": "Urban Carbon Emission", "p": ["Quantification of carbon emissions often relies on a top-down approach that multiplies annual national fuel consumption by an emissions factor. However, this method typically results in low spatial and temporal resolution. Carbon emissions are also observed directly, through stack emissions measurements or in-situ monitoring stations. In addition, advancements in satellite technology are rapidly enhancing global carbon emission monitoring capabilities.", "Urban areas are significant sources of carbon emissions due to various human activities. With increasing urbanization, there is a growing demand for high-resolution spatio-temporal technology to estimate carbon emissions. Particularly, emissions from road transportation, driven by rising traffic, have become one of the largest direct sources of carbon emissions. In this study, we aim to estimate carbon emissions by integrating traffic volume data with artificial intelligence. We will used a deep learning model to construct traffic volume in a city and calculate vehicle emissions on this data."], "refs": [], "prog": true, "img": "urban-9", "tr": ["도시 탄소 배출", "城市碳排放"], "rid": []}], "pubs": [8, 13, 26, 33, 39, 42, 50, 66, 75, 96, 100, 101, 120, 129, 137, 138, 141, 144, 151, 170], "ov": [["Thermal modeling", "열환경 모델링", "热环境建模", "Air temperature, Land surface temperature, Heatwave forecasting", "ic_urban_0"], ["Urban climate", "도시 기후", "城市气候", "Local climate zone, Urban heat island, Heat mitigation", "ic_urban_1"], ["Human activity", "인간 활동", "人类活动", "Traffic monitoring, Building energy use, Anthropogenic impacts", "ic_urban_2"]]}, "ghg": {"sec": [{"t": "Global Methane Estimation", "p": ["Methane (CH4) is a potent greenhouse gas, responsible for nearly 30% of the global warming effect since the pre-industrial era. Due to its significant role in climate change and its shorter atmospheric lifetime, accurately monitoring methane levels is critical for effective climate action. Precise methane data helps researchers understand sources, sinks, and overall atmospheric behavior, enabling more targeted and effective mitigation strategies.", "In this study, we aim to improve global methane concentration (XCH4) estimates by applying machine learning techniques. We use data from multiple satellite missions, including GOSAT, GOSAT-2, and Sentinel-5P, which provide important methane measurements but often contain biases due to differences in sensors, retrieval algorithms, and atmospheric conditions. To address these issues, we employ advanced machine learning approaches to integrate satellite data with ground-based observations from networks like TCCON. This allows us to correct biases and achieve more accurate methane concentration estimates on a global scale. The goal of our project is to deliver improved XCH4 data that can provide a more accurate understanding of global methane distributions, ultimately contributing to better-informed climate policies and strategies for mitigating methane emissions."], "refs": [], "prog": true, "img": "ghg-1", "tr": ["전 지구 메탄 추정", "全球甲烷估算"], "rid": []}, {"t": "Global High-Resolution Methane Plume Monitoring", "p": ["Methane is the second most significant anthropogenic greenhouse gas after carbon dioxide and a major contributor to near-term climate warming, making rapid emission reductions an effective climate mitigation strategy. Hyperspectral satellite observations enable detailed methane plume detection through characteristic absorption features, but the relative advantages and complementary roles of enhancement-based and radiance-based approaches for large-scale monitoring have not been fully established.", "In this study, we develop a dual-path deep learning framework that systematically compares enhancement-based and radiance-based methane plume detection using EMIT observations. The enhancement-based pathway provides accurate and stable plume segmentation, while the radiance-based pathway enables rapid detection directly from hyperspectral radiance without computationally intensive enhancement retrieval. We further evaluate the physical consistency of learned spatial and spectral features using explainable AI and assess architecture-level robustness with Tanager-1 observations. The framework is also applied to characterize the global distribution and major emission sectors of detected methane plumes. Together, these results support a scalable and sensor-agnostic framework for global methane monitoring."], "refs": ["Yang et al., 2026"], "prog": false, "img": "ghg-2", "tr": ["전 지구 고해상도 메탄 플룸 모니터링", "全球高分辨率甲烷羽流监测"], "rid": [11]}, {"t": "Long-term Global Carbon Dioxide (XCO2) Reconstruction", "p": ["Increasing awareness of greenhouse gases (GHGs) associated with climate change has highlighted the importance of monitoring atmospheric carbon dioxide (CO2), one of the most important GHGs. Industrial activities and fossil fuel combustion have driven a long-term increase in atmospheric CO2 concentrations, making continuous and accurate monitoring essential for understanding its environmental impacts. The column-averaged dry-air mole fraction of CO2 (XCO2) can be monitored using satellite observations, atmospheric reanalysis datasets, and ground-based observation networks, each of which has its own advantages and limitations. In recent years, satellite observations have become an important tool for monitoring atmospheric CO2. Nevertheless, satellite-based XCO2 still suffers from spatial data gaps, and its temporal coverage is always constrained by satellite launch dates (e.g., since 2014 for OCO-2).", "In this study, we focus on estimating spatial missing values in OCO-2 satellite observations to reconstruct a long-term, high-resolution global XCO2 dataset using an AI-based approach. Unlike previous studies that rely highly on reanalysis products, our framework leverages satellite observations and extends the reconstruction to approximately 11 years before the OCO-2 launch in 2014, improving spatial coverage and temporal continuity."], "refs": ["Hwang et al., 2026"], "prog": false, "img": "ghg-3", "tr": ["장기 전 지구 이산화탄소(XCO2) 재구축", "长期全球二氧化碳（XCO2）重建"], "rid": [14]}], "pubs": [5, 11, 14], "ov": [["Reconstruction", "재구축", "重建", "XCH4 estimation, Long-term XCO2 estimation", "ic_GHG_0"], ["Detection", "탐지", "检测", "CH4 plume detection", "ic_GHG_1"]]}, "cryosphere": {"sec": [{"t": "Deep learning-based Arctic melt pond detection", "p": ["Arctic melt ponds play a significant role in the reduction of sea ice extent and changes in the Arctic climate, and their accurate monitoring is important. However, their observation methods may suffer from reduced accuracy under varying weather conditions. To address this, different approaches capable of providing high-resolution data are needed. ICESat-2 measures surface elevation with high precision, enabling the detection of smaller melt ponds that were previously undetectable by CryoSat-2. Altimetry data can be utilized for polar research regardless of weather conditions, making it a valuable tool for studying the Arctic cryosphere.", "In this study, we developed a method to detect Arctic summer melt ponds using ICESat-2 altimetry data. For each ICESat-2 point, we analyzed the standard deviation of elevation and the number of photons within each segment to distinguish between sea ice and melt ponds. Since melt ponds have smoother surfaces than sea ice, they exhibit lower elevation variation and different photon counts, which were used to classify melt ponds covered by water and those covered by ice. The proposed detection method was validated using Sentinel-2 optical imagery and effectively identified various types of melt ponds."], "refs": ["Han et al., 2021"], "prog": false, "img": "polar-1", "tr": ["딥러닝 기반 북극 융빙호 탐지", "基于深度学习的北极融池检测"], "rid": [null]}, {"t": "Deep learning-based Arctic SIC prediction", "p": ["Accurate prediction of Arctic sea-ice concentration (SIC) is essential for maritime planning and climate monitoring, yet traditional numerical and statistical models often struggle—especially during rapid summer ice decline. Recent deep-learning approaches offer a promising alternative by better capturing nonlinear relationships and spatiotemporal patterns in SIC variability.", "Kim et al. (2020) developed a CNN-based monthly SIC prediction model and showed that it outperformed a Random Forest model and a monthly trend baseline, with particularly strong skill during extreme rapid-decline cases.", "Kim et al. (2025) further advanced this direction by proposing a U-Net-based pan-Arctic monthly SIC forecasting framework for 3–12-month lead times, demonstrating consistently stable performance against operational forecasts (C3S), damped anomaly persistence, and other deep-learning baselines. Their analysis also indicated that antecedent SIC and surface temperature are key contributors to long-lead predictability, while solar radiation and winds can be more influential in thin-ice regions."], "refs": ["Kim et al., 2020", "Kim et al., 2025"], "prog": false, "img": "polar-2", "tr": ["딥러닝 기반 북극 해빙 농도 예측", "基于深度学习的北极海冰密集度预测"], "rid": [140, 19]}, {"t": "Quantification of sea ice characteristics", "p": ["Sea ice indices such as sea ice concentration (SIC) play a key role in monitoring climate change. However, it does not fully capture the vulnerability of sea ice to melting, especially under conditions of floe fragmentation. To address this limitation, we introduce a novel metric—the floe fragmentation index (FFI)—designed to quantify the degree of fragmentation of sea ice. FFI increases as sea ice becomes more fragmented. Even under identical SIC conditions (e.g., 50%), the reference FFI captured distinct differences in floe structure, demonstrating its ability to represent fragmentation more explicitly than SIC.", "At the point of sea ice disappearance within this area, no early signs were observed in SIC, whereas FFI did reveal such signals. In particular, under conditions where the sea ice was highly fragmented and thus more likely to drift away or melt, SIC remained close to 100%, while FFI captured the relatively severe fragmentation of the sea ice. The FFI complemented SIC by capturing fragmentation changes, potentially allowing earlier detection of SIC change in the melting seasons."], "refs": ["Kim et al., 2025"], "prog": false, "img": "polar-3", "tr": ["해빙 특성 정량화", "海冰特征量化"], "rid": [19]}, {"t": "Deep learning-based Antarctic SIC prediction", "p": ["Antarctic sea-ice concentration has declined sharply since 2015, and robust long-term prediction is required to determine whether this change is a temporary fluctuation or a persistent shift. Traditionally, Antarctic SIC prediction has relied on statistical methods and physics-based numerical models. Recently, deep-learning approaches have emerged as computationally efficient alternatives with competitive performance. However, many existing deep-learning studies focus on a single climate variable, which limits their ability to represent the complex ocean–atmosphere interactions and sea-ice dynamics that govern Antarctic sea-ice variability.", "To address this gap, this study proposes a deep-learning–based long-term forecasting framework that integrates diverse oceanic and atmospheric predictors. We employ a spatiotemporal forecasting model well suited for time-series imagery and further enhance spatial details in a post-processing stage by applying residual correction to refine fine-scale structures and ice-edge features."], "refs": [], "prog": true, "img": "polar-4", "tr": ["딥러닝 기반 남극 해빙 농도 예측", "基于深度学习的南极海冰密集度预测"], "rid": []}], "pubs": [19, 35, 140, 146, 165, 184, 188, 197, 203], "ov": [["Sea ice characterization", "해빙 특성 분석", "海冰特征识别", "Landfast sea ice, Ice lead & melt pond, Floe size", "ic_cryosphere_0"], ["Sea ice quantification", "해빙 정량화", "海冰量化", "Melt pond fraction, Sea ice concentration (SIC), Floe Fragmentation Index", "ic_cryosphere_1"], ["Sea ice prediction", "해빙 예측", "海冰预测", "Short-term and long-term SIC prediction", "ic_cryosphere_2"]]}, "drought": {"sec": [{"t": "Drought Monitoring", "p": ["Drought is one of the most complex natural disasters, making it difficult to clearly define its onset, intensity, and scope. Based on the impacts it causes, droughts are generally classified into four types: meteorological, agricultural, hydrological, and socioeconomic.", "To represent drought information, we utilize satellite data, meteorological data, and other relevant sources to conduct large-scale drought monitoring. Through satellite observations, we monitor vegetation vitality, rainfall patterns, surface temperature, and other environmental factors. By analyzing this data, we quantify the stress caused by specific types of droughts (Park et al., 2017; Park et al., 2020, …).", "Furthermore, to provide comprehensive drought information, we are developing new drought monitoring methods and indices tailored to user needs (Son et al., 2021). This method considers multiple interdependent variables on a continental scale, allowing users to focus on the type of drought most relevant to their interests.", "Additionally, our research delves into the causes of drought and explores key indicators that represent drought phenomena, aiming to enhance understanding and response strategies."], "refs": ["Park et al., 2017", "Park et al., 2020", "Son et al., 2021"], "prog": false, "img": "drought-2", "tr": ["가뭄 모니터링", "干旱监测"], "rid": [180, 128, 119]}, {"t": "Drought Forecasting", "p": ["Drought forecasts are critical for managing drought-related damage effectively and providing timely, relevant information to decision-makers. To address this, we are developing drought forecast models that blend the temporal patterns of drought indices with numerical model  forecast outputs using advanced deep learning techniques. These models are designed to provide forecasts on both short-term (~days) and (sub)seasonal time scales.", "Key aspects of our research include the development of week-to-seasonal drought forecast models, the application of artificial intelligence techniques, particularly deep learning, and the integration of remote sensing data with model outputs to enhance the accuracy and reliability of drought forecasts (Park et al., 2018; Park et al., 2020; In progress)"], "refs": ["Park et al., 2018", "Park et al., 2020"], "prog": true, "img": "drought-3", "tr": ["가뭄 예측", "干旱预测"], "rid": [171, 128]}, {"t": "Soil Moisture Retrieval", "p": ["Soil Moisture (SM), the water content held in the spaces between soil particles, plays a critical role in maintaining ecosystems and agricultural productivity. One of the information sources of the SM is satellite remote sensing. Especially, microwave remote sensing provides and unique opportunity to retrieve SM from space.", "We trying to improve those SM data accuracy (Lee et al., 2022) and availability (Lee et al., 2024) by incorporating three main data sources—ground measurement, satellite measurement, and numerical models—with state-of-the-art deep-learning (DL) algorithms.", "Furthermore, we trying to understand how the DL model works—Generally regarded as a “black-box”—by inversely tracking DL models with physical equations (Lee at al., 2024; TGRS).", "We anticipate that our study results can improve our understanding of global water and carbon cycles."], "refs": ["Lee et al., 2022", "Lee et al., 2024"], "prog": true, "img": "drought-4", "tr": ["토양수분 산출", "土壤水分反演"], "rid": [97, 41]}], "pubs": [10, 16, 41, 44, 97, 119, 126, 128, 143, 150, 171, 179, 180, 181, 189, 196], "ov": [["Drought monitoring", "가뭄 모니터링", "干旱监测", "Specific drought types, Integrated drought", "ic_Drought_0"], ["Drought forecasting", "가뭄 예측", "干旱预测", "Short-term (~days), Sub-seasonal to seasonal", "ic_Drought_1"], ["Soil moisture retrieval", "토양수분 산출", "土壤水分反演", "Data harmonization, Radiative transfer model parameterization", "ic_Drought_2"]]}, "wildfire": {"sec": [{"t": "Fire risk assessment", "p": ["Wildfires can cause extensive damage, including deforestation and environmental pollution, even from a single event. Restoring affected areas requires substantial time and resources. Therefore, assessing wildfire risk in advance is essential for effective preparedness and response. Most existing wildfire risk indices primarily rely on weather variables associated with ignition, while comprehensive analyses incorporating diverse environmental factors remain limited.", "Kang et al. (2019, 2020) addressed this limitation by developing an hourly forest fire risk index (HFRI) at 1 km resolution for South Korea. The index integrates accessibility, fuel, temporal, and meteorological factors using the CatBoost machine learning algorithm. HFRI was constructed from an ensemble framework combining integrated and meteorological models, enabling more spatially detailed and seasonally stable simulations compared to earlier indices. Feature contributions analyzed using Shapley Additive Explanations (SHAP) revealed that relative humidity, elevation, road density, and population density were key drivers, underscoring the critical role of accessibility in human-induced wildfire risk."], "refs": ["Kang et al., 2019", "Kang et al., 2020"], "prog": false, "img": "wildfire-3", "tr": ["산불 위험도 평가", "火险评估"], "rid": [null, null]}, {"t": "Fire weather forecasting", "p": ["To minimize the damage caused by forest fires, accurate risk forecasting is essential. Currently, South Korea’s forest fire risk forecasting system provides only short-term predictions, limited to 48 hours. However, the increasing frequency of both small- and large-scale forest fires has resulted in substantial damage, rendering short-term forecasts insufficient for effective response. This highlights the need for mid-range forecasting to enable more proactive wildfire management.", "Park et al. (2022) developed 7-day mid-range forest fire risk prediction models for South Korea using a random forest algorithm. The models incorporated weather forecasts, historical and current Fire Risk Index (FRI), elevation, forest fire hazard ratings, and drought indices as input variables. The results demonstrated high predictive accuracy and strong agreement with observed wildfire occurrences. Notably, incorporating historical FRI values yielded more accurate predictions than using FRI trends, regardless of the drought index applied."], "refs": ["Park et al., 2022"], "prog": false, "img": "wildfire-4", "tr": ["산불 기상 예측", "火险天气预报"], "rid": [83]}, {"t": "Active fire detection", "p": ["To minimize wildfire damage, rapid and coordinated responses enabled by early detection and real-time monitoring are essential. Geostationary satellite imagery, with its high temporal resolution, facilitates near-real-time detection over large areas. However, most existing wildfire detection algorithms rely on threshold-based approaches applied to satellite imagery, limiting their spatiotemporal generalizability. Our goal is to develop a robust real-time wildfire monitoring system that operates reliably across diverse environmental conditions by integrating AI-based models with satellite imagery, weather data, and observational variables.", "Following the work of Jang et al. (2019), who reduced false alarms through machine learning-based post-processing, numerous studies have incorporated AI techniques into wildfire detection. Kang et al. (2022) emphasized the importance of spatial information by comparing random forest and convolutional neural networks, while Lee et al. (2023) proposed efficient deep learning approaches through systematic analysis of state-of-the-art architectures. More recently, Kang et al. (2023) introduced a dual-module framework to independently process satellite imagery and auxiliary variables, and Sung et al. (2024, 2025) applied techniques to remove environmental trends from satellite observations to enhance detection robustness."], "refs": ["Jang et al., 2019", "Kang et al., 2022", "Lee et al., 2023", "Kang et al., 2023", "Sung et al., 2024", "Sung et al., 2025"], "prog": false, "img": "wildfire-5", "tr": ["활성 산불 탐지", "活跃火点检测"], "rid": [160, 84, 75, 67, 46, 30]}, {"t": "Fire radiative power estimation", "p": ["Satellite remote sensing provides essential information on fire radiative power (FRP), a key variable for estimating biomass burning emissions and understanding fire dynamics. However, FRP evaluation remains challenging due to limited field validation data and differences in sensor characteristics across satellite platforms. Previous intercomparison studies have attempted to assess FRP uncertainty, but they are often limited in scope and influenced by differences in fire detection capabilities.", "To address these limitations, Kang et al. (2026) conducts a global evaluation of FRP from three satellite sensors—MODIS, VIIRS, and SLSTR—by harmonizing fire clusters while preserving sensor-specific detection characteristics. In addition, extended triple collocation (ETC) analysis is applied to assess FRP consistency without relying on reference data. The results highlight overall agreement among sensors, with regional differences reflecting variations in fire characteristics and detection sensitivity."], "refs": ["Kang et al., 2026"], "prog": false, "img": "wildfire-6", "tr": ["화재 복사 강도 추정", "火辐射功率估算"], "rid": [6]}, {"t": "Burn scar analysis", "p": ["Burn scar analysis is essential not only for delineating damaged areas but also for guiding forest and ecosystem restoration, preventing secondary hazards such as landslides, and providing critical data for climate studies. Wildfire damage assessment can be conducted using either optical or synthetic aperture radar (SAR) imagery. Although optical imagery generally provides high accuracy, its performance is often hindered by cloud and smoke interference, reducing timeliness and reliability. In contrast, SAR enables all-weather data acquisition; however, it is subject to higher uncertainty due to inherent speckle noise, necessitating additional preprocessing and advanced AI-based techniques.", "Sim et al. (2020) developed a machine learning model integrating Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 data to estimate wildfire severity. Lee et al. (2021) utilized Sentinel-1 SAR data combined with principal component analysis (PCA) and K-means clustering to estimate burned areas, achieving high accuracy when validated against local reference data. More recently, Rana et al. (2026) evaluated five U-Net variants for Sentinel-1 SAR-based burned area mapping. By analyzing their performance under different environmental and observational conditions, these studies identify conditions in which specific components underperform and provides insights into strategies for overcoming these limitations."], "refs": ["Sim et al., 2020", "Lee et al., 2021", "Rana et al., 2026"], "prog": false, "img": "wildfire-7", "tr": ["산불 피해지 분석", "火烧迹地分析"], "rid": [null, 120, 17]}, {"t": "Emission estimation", "p": ["Wildfires emit substantial amounts of air pollutants and greenhouse gases, degrading local air quality and contributing to global climate change. Emission estimates derived from satellite-based fire radiative energy provide essential baseline data for air quality assessment and climate research.", "Kang et al. (2024) and Kang and Im (2024) utilized geostationary satellite imagery to address the underestimation issues in existing emission products and investigated the impacts of wildfire emissions on surrounding air quality."], "refs": ["Kang et al., 2024", "Kang & Im, 2024"], "prog": false, "img": "wildfire-8", "tr": ["산불 배출량 추정", "排放量估算"], "rid": [51, 51]}], "pubs": [4, 6, 9, 17, 18, 30, 46, 51, 55, 67, 73, 75, 83, 84, 120, 130, 150, 157, 160, 166, 192, 207], "ov": [["Pre-fire phase", "산불 발생 전", "火灾前", "Fire risk assessment, Fire weather forecasting", "ic_wildfire_0"], ["Active-fire phase", "산불 진행 중", "火灾中", "Active fire detection, Fire radiative power estimation", "ic_wildfire_1"], ["Post-fire phase", "산불 발생 후", "火灾后", "Burn scar analysis, Emission estimation", "ic_wildfire_2"]]}};
